<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Nothing leaves this phone (SPEC §7.9, second page).

    Everything the sentence lists — the route, the station, the address looked
    up — is drawn INSIDE the phone, at full contrast. Outside, a single thin
    dashed thread leaves, and all it reaches is a bike count: the only request
    that goes out in everyday use.

    The asymmetry is the whole argument, so it is drawn rather than stated: one
    thread against a screenful, dashed rather than solid, because it is an
    occasional question and not a pipe. Drawing an outgoing arrow at all was
    the risk here — at a glance an arrow leaving a phone reads as data
    escaping. What keeps the meaning right is the weight: the phone owns the
    drawing, the thread is a hairline, and it ends on a bike, not on a cloud.

    The pin, the disc and the walk dashes are the same shapes the map and the
    journey screen use: what stays here is exactly what the rest of the
    application shows.
-->
